Z||000000||V8Fiero||05-06-2001||08:52 PM||||The one that designed the brake caliper piston tool or the one that designed that G** damn little retaining ring on the brake pad!!!!!???<P><BR>Replaced both rear wheel bearing hub assemblies today. Not sure how many of you have done brake work on a Fiero, but it is  a royal pain in the you know what getting the piston pushed/turned in far enough to get the calipers back on. <P>Bought the little tool to turn the piston in and everything but still a pain.....<P>Boy, did those new bearings make a difference. Rear end is really tight.....<P>Just had to vent a little now that the job is accomplished<P>Scott<P>||||reg||8
Z||000001||Tigger||05-06-2001||10:07 PM||||Yep, I replaced rear brake pads on mine yesterday.  UH, it sucked too.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>I agree, the caliper is a royal pain the butt to press back in.  And the clip thingy, who came up with that???||||reg||1
Z||000002||FieroLisa||05-06-2001||10:21 PM||||yesterday must have been brake pad changing day then! I didn't know how to till yesterday when I watched, but it didn't seem *that* bad...||||reg||1
Z||000003||WKDFIRO||05-06-2001||10:53 PM||||LOL!<P>I just finished working on the brakes for a friend of mine! She went metal to metal on the front (GRRRRRRRRRRRRINNNNNND!) and I got to find out how much fun and freaking expensive 88 brake rotors are! I spent the whole day trying to find rotors that were on the shelf (I thought I would scream if I heard "special order one more time!) and weren't over 90 bucks (Pep Boys wanted $122 EACH!!! EEEEK!)I ended up snagging a new rotor for $65. Since my friend was on a budget I got the other rotor from the recycle yard on the condition that I could at least turn them. They were gauranteed to have at least one turn on them so I happily left (RAN) with a good rotor for $35 and got it turned.<BR>    Never had any problems with pushing the pistons back in (I have an 85 GT that Ive done the brakes on many times now)with a C-clamp (while watching the brake fluid levels) always got me squared away!<BR>    Oh and those clips....GAD! I know of what you speak! Pontiac did come out with better clips for the 84-87 but they were still a pain!<BR>    Congratulations on getting the brakes done yourself. I always have the good feeling of self reliance when Im done with those kind of jobs on my Fiero!<p>[This message has been edited by WKDFIRO (edited 05-06-2001).]||||reg||14
Z||000004||Loki||05-06-2001||11:17 PM||||People have problems doing brakes?   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  Ive done both rear and front not knowing what th ehll I was doing....Easy I thought....As for the caliper pistons..Get a big C-clamp..Works just fine.....<BR><P>------------------<BR>Loki<BR>ICQ# 16477998<BR>www.cox-internet.com/fiero  !!!UPDATED!!!||||reg||12
Z||000005||FieroLisa||05-06-2001||11:36 PM||||loki- agreed on the c clamp||||reg||1
Z||000006||V8Fiero||05-07-2001||01:18 AM||||Now I have tried the c clamp in the past. Has worked on the front but not the rears. Couldnt budge them......||||reg||1
Z||000007||pontiacs4me||05-07-2001||02:36 AM||||I couldnt figure out how to compress the rear calipers, I figured they were seized when I couldnt get the pistons in with a vice, so I bought new ones.  They are already all the way in.   And damn the rears are expensive.  the fronts are cheap.  I just replaced everything.  And the car is still hard as hell to stop when you need to.  My friend andy has a 85 mercury marquis that weighs about 5000 lbs, and it can outstop the fiero anyday.  ||||reg||1
Z||000008||Carrolles||05-07-2001||11:46 AM||||To compress the rears remove the parking brake cable lever.  As you push the piston back in the shaft will turn.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Spektyr||05-07-2001||01:27 PM||||In the vaguely near future I'm planning a brake job that will include four new vented rotors and calipers to go with them.  I figure I can just unbolt everything and pull the rotors off with the calipers still attached, saving myself a lot of work.<P>Am I right?||||reg||1
Z||000010||Ken Wittlief||05-07-2001||02:31 PM||||no, the calipers bolt to the part of the wheel housing that doent turn, unless you want to replace everything back there.<P>  You have to take the calipers off to get the rotor off - its not hard, the #50 torques bolts came out easily on mine, but I did have to push the brake hose out of the way for the top one, and the ebrake spring out of the way for the bottom one.<p>[This message has been edited by Ken Wittlief (edited 05-07-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000011||fldevel||05-07-2001||07:29 PM||||fiero hard to stop ??hmm .. try pushing down harder  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> .. mybreaks work VERRY well to the point whare if i slam on my breaks ur eyeballs feal like there coming outa the sockets ...||||reg||1
Z||000012||StuGood||05-08-2001||06:49 PM||||Fiero brakes are difficult the first time, just like almost any job.  After that, you know what to do, and it's relatively easy!<P>First (I think?) time I rebuilt a rear caliper, I had the caliper off, and put some rags in the caliper to catch the piston.  Next I set it on the floor (E-brake shaft up), and applied compressed air to the fitting to shoot the piston out, like you normally do.<P>I was standing over it, looking down on the E-brake shaft.  I thought the piston would shoot out the bottom, but of course the E-brake shaft had other ideas.  The E-brake shaft moved upwards, out of its hole, and brake fluid squirted right into my eyes.<P>Was Stu wearing goggles? Nope.  Stu learn something? Yeahmmp. Stu more smarter now.<P>After I cried the brake fluid out of my eyes, I laughed until I almost cried again  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> !!       ||||reg||10
Z||000013||Spektyr||05-09-2001||03:11 PM||||Maybe I'm not getting it, but if you're going to replace the rotors and calipers anyway, couldn't I just unbolt the calipers and then pull the rotors off with the calipers still on them?  No worrying about releasing the calipers, simply pull the whole assembly out together?||||reg||1
Z||000014||Tigger||05-09-2001||03:30 PM||||After you remove the caliper bolts, the caliper should come off the rotor just by jiggling it or by lightly tapping it with a hammer.<P>I've never tried removing the caliper and rotor locked together like that.  I imagine it's possible.<p>[This message has been edited by Tigger (edited 05-09-2001).]||||reg||1
